;; FH-PROPS-005 — a published view carries a props schema, and a gate says so.
;;
;; Props schemas are optional in the grammar. That is the posture, and the whole
;; point of it is that the mandate falls somewhere ELSE: on the surfaces where a
;; contract crosses a boundary. A published, reusable view is read by callers its
;; author will never meet — through a catalogue entry, an editor completion, an
;; agent authoring a call site — and for a shipped control the declaration IS the
;; contract. A rule that is policy and nothing else is a rule that erodes, so it
;; is a gate.
;;
;; `:catalogue` is the roster the gate governs: the views the public door itself
;; publishes. It is named here rather than derived, so ADDING a published view is
;; a deliberate edit to this fixture and never a quiet inheritance of the
;; exemption — a new door view with no row fails the gate as surely as one with a
;; row and no schema.
;;
;; Visibility is decided by the publishing surface, never by a `:public`
;; declaration option: a flag would be a second, weaker statement of something
;; the build already knows.
;;
;; `:closed?` records which way each published view went, because the honest
;; answer is not the same for all three. `v/route-link` forwards every
;; unrecognised key to its `<a>`, so a closed roster would be a contract it
;; breaks on its first `:aria-label`; it declares itself open instead. Declaring
;; a schema and declaring it CLOSED are two decisions, and the mandate is only
;; the first.
{:fh/id "FH-PROPS-005"
 :catalogue
 {:re-frame.freehand/markup
  {:closed? true
   :why "one prop, `:value`; a caller reaching for a second has misread the boundary"}
  :re-frame.freehand/error-boundary
  {:closed? true
   :why "the option roster its own docstring calls closed, said once as data"}
  :re-frame.freehand/route-link
  {:closed? false
   :why "every unrecognised key forwards to the anchor, so open is the truthful declaration"}}}
